WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
THE EXPLOSION OF TEMU:
By now, I’m sure most of you have heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:08.680
about Temu. They’re the sketchy online retailer&nbsp;
that sells counterfeits and unbelievably cheap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.720
items. If you’re not familiar with Temu, just&nbsp;
take a look at what happens if you look up Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:21.280
Watch on Temu. You get all these results that only&nbsp;
cost $10 to $25 and that’s not just for the band,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.440
it’s for the entire watch itself. It’s the&nbsp;
same thing if you look up Apple Airpods. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:32.520
get a bunch of knockoffs that are as cheap as $4.&nbsp;
Obviously, these counterfeits don’t quite stack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:38.560
up to the real deal but for the price, they’re&nbsp;
actually insane. Oftentimes, you can’t even tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.120
which one is fake and which one is real even when&nbsp;
they’re side by side. It’s not just electronics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.040
that are cheap on Temu either. Everything from&nbsp;
shows and sweatshirts to power tools and pet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.680
supplies can be found for ridiculously cheap&nbsp;
prices on Temu. And if you think all of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.680 --> 00:01:00.760
seems too good to be true, well then, it’s because&nbsp;
it is. The only reason Temu is able to sell all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:07.120
of these items for so cheap is because they take a&nbsp;
massive loss on each and every item sold. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.920
according to Wired, Temu is losing an average&nbsp;
of $30 on every single order which added up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:17.520
to a total loss of over $3 billion last year.&nbsp;
And I think now would be a good time to mention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:23.000
that Temu wasn’t even launched till September&nbsp;
of 2022. So, they haven’t even been in business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:29.960
for 18 months at this point, and they’re burn&nbsp;
rate is already over $3 billion per year. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:34.440
there is method behind all the madness.&nbsp;
Thanks to Temu’s promotional prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:40.600
they’ve exploded in popularity. Within a matter&nbsp;
of 18 months, they’ve been able to take on Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:48.680
and all the other physical retailers go from 0&nbsp;
to 89th most visited website within the US and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:54.280
148th most visited website in the world pulling&nbsp;
in over 400 million visits every single month.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:00.880
For perspective, they rank higher than established&nbsp;
media outlets like Fox Business, NBC News, CNBC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.720
and AP News. And that’s just their website.&nbsp;
Temu is often the most downloaded app within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:11.480
the US on both the iOS app store and the&nbsp;
Google Play Store in any given month. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:15.440
who exactly are the people behind Temu, and&nbsp;
will they actually be able to make it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:27.902
the other side, or are these losses gonna&nbsp;
end up killing them from the inside out.

00:02:27.902 --> 00:02:29.000
HUMBLE BEGINNINGS:
Taking a look back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:35.160
the story of Temu takes us back to a middle class&nbsp;
Chinese man named Colin Huang or Huang Zheng.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:41.560
Colin was born in Zhejiang, China on January 1,&nbsp;
1980 to two factory workers. As you could guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.000
Colin really didn’t start off with much and he&nbsp;
would follow the path of any hard working Asian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.880
immigrant. He would study his butt off and get&nbsp;
into one of the most prestigious universities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.960
in China: Zhejiang University where he majored in&nbsp;
computer science. From there, he would turn around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.560
and attend the University of Wisconsin, where he&nbsp;
would obtain a masters in computer science. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.240
background allowed him to score internships at&nbsp;
Microsoft and Google and receive job offers from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:10.800
several top tier tech companies including&nbsp;
Microsoft, Oracle, IBM, and Google who at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.800
the time was just a small startup. But, on the&nbsp;
recommendation of one of his friends/mentors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:20.640
William Ding who is a multi deca billionaire&nbsp;
today himself, Colin would end up joining Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.880
as a full time software engineer at Google in&nbsp;
2004. This would turn out to be a brilliant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:31.400
move as Google would go public that same year&nbsp;
and they would reach a $100 billion market cap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:37.200
just 1 year later. This meant that Colin’s stock&nbsp;
compensation went to the moon and he would become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:43.000
a multi millionaire in his mid 20s. For most Asian&nbsp;
immigrants, this is where the arc would naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.720
come to a conclusion. From here, they would&nbsp;
just work up the corporate ladder into the upper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:53.600
echelons of management and executive leadership,&nbsp;
and that would pretty much be it. But, Colin would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.240
become part of a growing group of Asian immigrants&nbsp;
who were interested in bringing American tech back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:03.800
home to China. Technically, this was actually his&nbsp;
official job at Google itself. He was supposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:08.480
to help Google with their expansion into China,&nbsp;
but this didn’t work out all that well as Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:12.960
wasn’t really able to compete against Chinese&nbsp;
giants like Baidu. Colin saw the writing on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:19.000
wall by 2007 and he would leave Google to create&nbsp;
his own e commerce startup called Ouku in China.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:24.360
Ouku was just you’re run of the mill e commerce&nbsp;
electronics shop. Thanks to how big the interente&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.880
is, Ouku was able to achieve moderate success&nbsp;
in the millions of dollars but it didn’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.920
hold a candle against real giants like Alibaba,&nbsp;
JD.com, and Tencent. As such, Colin would end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:40.720
flipping Ouku for $2.2 million in 2010 and start&nbsp;
looking for a new angle within the e commerce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.360
market. This sort of spiritual journey ended&nbsp;
up taking multiple years and one point he even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:51.720
considered moving back to the United States and&nbsp;
just opening a tech hedge fund. But eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:58.120
he would find his unique angle in 2015 thanks to&nbsp;
the rise of mobile social media. Colin noticed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:03.600
that there were massive online retailers like&nbsp;
Amazon and Alibaba and massive social media giants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.560
like Facebook and Tencent, but there was no one&nbsp;
who was trying to do both at the same time. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:15.720
this would end up being the unique spin he would&nbsp;
integrate into his new ecommerce business in 2015.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:20.560
The goal of this business was to gamify shopping&nbsp;
like never before by combining the most addictive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:32.546
parts of shopping and the most addictive parts&nbsp;
of social media and he would call it Pinduoduo.

00:05:32.546 --> 00:05:34.960
MAKING A NAME:
Pinduoduo was exactly the e-commerce breakthrough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:40.160
that Colin was looking for that could take on&nbsp;
the Chinese giants? What did Pinduoduo do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:46.120
was so unique you ask? Well, like Temu, it offered&nbsp;
rock bottom prices but instead of the price being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:52.080
subsidized by Pinduoduo, it was subsidized&nbsp;
by scale. For example, users might see an ad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.320
about how they could get groceries like fruits and&nbsp;
vegetables for half the price. There was just one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:02.720
catch. To get the groceries for half the price,&nbsp;
you needed to recruit 50 or a 100 people to buy it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:09.000
with you. The more people you recruited, the lower&nbsp;
the price. And given that Pinduoduo wasn’t selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.840
smartphones and computers or something, and&nbsp;
instead selling simple cheap everyday goods that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:18.060
everybody needed, it was quite easy to recruit&nbsp;
people because the ask was so small and straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:22.280
forward. You could share it with your friends and&nbsp;
family or the people in your apartment or your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:29.240
classmates and colleagues. Together, you could all&nbsp;
save 25 or 50% on everyday goods, and on the flip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:36.720
side, Pinduoduo just got 50 or a 100 repeating&nbsp;
customers by advertising to just one person. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:42.760
that’s just the first cycle, imagine what happens&nbsp;
when each of those 50 people tell 50 more people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:48.800
The network effect simply becomes ridiculous and&nbsp;
that’s exactly what happened. Before you knew it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:54.160
Pinduoduo would become a sizeable player within&nbsp;
the Chinese e-commerce market. As of 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:58.800
they stood at 17.4% market share across the&nbsp;
country. And Colin would simply fuel this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:03.720
fire with practices that he learned from Google.&nbsp;
For example, instead of trying to pay engineers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:08.560
as little as possible, he would pay them 30%&nbsp;
more than they would earn at Alibaba and 60%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:14.280
more than they would earn at JD.com. Also, instead&nbsp;
of hiring candidates with decades of experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:19.560
Colin would hire fresh college graduates who&nbsp;
were hungry for success. And I think this chart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:24.760
tells you everything you need to know regarding&nbsp;
what happened next. Pinduoduo would essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:31.360
grow from 0 to $18 billion worth of annual revenue&nbsp;
within a matter of 6 years. And it was from this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:37.880
mountain top that Colin would launch Temu. The&nbsp;
concept behind Temu is extremely simple. It’s no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:42.800
secret that basically all the listings on Amazon&nbsp;
and Shopify are just highly marked up low quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:48.760
goods from China, and people are not only starting&nbsp;
to notice but they’re getting frustrated. Why pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:54.760
$20 for something that you can get for $5 on&nbsp;
Alibaba? Atleast then, the quality will match&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.760 --> 00:08:00.240
the price. There’s only one problem with this&nbsp;
logic though. The average westerner isn’t familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:06.840
with Alibaba or Aliexpress. All they know is that&nbsp;
their Amazon purchases are shit, and that’s were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:12.120
Temu comes in. Temu’s main goal has been to bridge&nbsp;
this knowledge gap and show people that they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:17.800
buy the same stuff they get on Amazon for way&nbsp;
cheaper. Now, I should mention that Alibaba has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:24.080
been trying to do this with Aliexpress since 2010,&nbsp;
but let’s just say that Alibaba never understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:36.622
social media the same way that Temu does which&nbsp;
brings us into Temu’s brilliant launch strategy.

00:08:36.622 --> 00:08:38.680
MARKET DOMINATION:
Temu’s strategy was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:44.720
extraordinarily simple. Instead of spending&nbsp;
millions on Google ads or influencer sponsorships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:50.040
Temu decided to spend millions on brilliantly&nbsp;
positioned giveaways. Temu basically went out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:55.400
and purchased thousands if not tens of thousands&nbsp;
of legitimate products that westerners wanted like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:59.480
Nintendo Switches. They didn’t just straight&nbsp;
up give these away though. If they had just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:05.440
run a giveaway, no one would’ve batted an eye. It&nbsp;
would’ve just been seen as an obvious promotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:12.400
event. Instead, what Temu did was price these&nbsp;
products at ridiculously low prices. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:18.040
they offered the Switch for $7. All of a sudden,&nbsp;
Temu was able to create an insane amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:24.080
mystery and intrigue as to what was going on&nbsp;
here. Is it a scam? Is it a counterfeit? Were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:30.920
these units stolen? You know, all those normal&nbsp;
natural questions. As such, 99.99% of the people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:39.160
who saw this probably chuckled and moved on. But&nbsp;
0.01% thought: hey this is probably a scam but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:44.800
it’s only $7. I’ll just use my burner PayPal&nbsp;
account to pay for it. If I get it, I get it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:50.000
if I don’t, I don’t. And to their surprise,&nbsp;
they actually end up receiving the Switch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:55.360
and it’s not a counterfeit or stolen unit&nbsp;
either, it’s straight up legit. This only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:59.960
has to happen to a few hundred people before it&nbsp;
becomes international headlines and millions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:05.120
people are talking about it on social media.&nbsp;
And that’s exactly what happened. Honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:10.680
credit to Temu for running such a genius marketing&nbsp;
campaign. In terms of the ratio between exposure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:16.960
and dollars spent, this was probably one of the&nbsp;
best campaigns in history. Basically overnight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:22.080
hundreds of millions of people now knew about&nbsp;
Temu. And when they check out the website or app,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:27.400
they’ll see that they can’t quite get a $7 Switch&nbsp;
every day of the week, but they can get products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:33.720
that are way cheaper than on Amazon or American&nbsp;
retailers, and naturally a lot of people are quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:39.400
interested. And honestly, I think that’s great&nbsp;
because it’s about time that we stop overpaying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:44.880
for junk on Amazon and dropshipping stores.&nbsp;
In terms of the viability of Temu, normally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:50.000
burning $3 billion per year would be an extremely&nbsp;
dangerous game to play even with a bunch of VC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:56.480
capital. But, luckily for Temu, they’re backed&nbsp;
by the giant that is Pinduoduo who is currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.480 --> 00:11:04.200
worth $128 billion. So, it’s highly likely that&nbsp;
Temu is here to stay and they’re only gonna grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:09.800
in popularity. With that being said though, if&nbsp;
you’re gonna buy on Temu, you should be wary of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:16.680
2 things. Number 1, Temu is not a scam but they do&nbsp;
have terrible customer support because the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:21.200
platform is essentially just a bunch of Chinese&nbsp;
factories and warehouses that don’t really care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:27.080
about the customer experience. So, it’s definitely&nbsp;
a possibility that your order get’s lost and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:33.200
have no recourse. So, definitely be careful about&nbsp;
how much you spend on Temu and what exactly you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:38.920
buy. And as for number 2? Well, I’m not trying&nbsp;
to stereotype here, but Temu is almost certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:44.120
installing spyware, malware, tracking a bunch&nbsp;
of your data, and who knows what else. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:49.640
Pinduoduo was removed from the Google Play Store&nbsp;
for exactly those reasons. With that being said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:54.520
though, if it’s not Temu that’s tracking you,&nbsp;
it’s gonna be Google, Facebook, and all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.520 --> 00:12:00.120
other American tech giants. So, it’s really up to&nbsp;
you as to whether you’re ok with a Chinese company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:07.320
doing the same thing. But either way, this is only&nbsp;
the beginning of Temu and Chinese tech in general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:13.320
and it’s only gonna get crazier from here. TikTok&nbsp;
has already started implementing some brilliant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:18.120
strategies to ensure that they never get banned&nbsp;
from the US. Check out this video to learn more.

